          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Ludwig van Beethoven's Early Years</strong><br>\nLudwig van Beethoven was born in Bonn, Germany, in 1770, into a family of musicians. Displaying musical talent from a young age, Beethoven was taught by his father Johann and later by composer Christian Gottlob Neefe. Under Neefe's tutelage, Beethoven published his first work at age 12. His formative years were spent in a rigorous musical environment that prepared him for his later career.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Move to Vienna</strong><br>\nIn his early twenties, Beethoven moved to Vienna, which was then the heart of the musical world. Originally planning to study with Mozart, he instead became a student of Joseph Haydn after Mozart's death. Vienna offered Beethoven countless opportunities to display his talent as a pianist and composer. It was here that his reputation began to flourish.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Establishing a Musical Career</strong><br>\nBeethoven quickly established himself as a virtuoso pianist and an innovative composer in Vienna. His early works were marked by a command of the classical style, yet he already began pushing the boundaries of musical traditions. Notable patrons, such as Prince Lichnowsky, supported his work, and his public performances won him acclaim.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
