            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Hearing Loss and Personal Struggle</strong><br>\nAround 1801, Beethoven began to lose his hearing, a devastating development for a musician. This personal struggle deeply affected his life and work, leading to periods of despair, which is reflected in the emotional depth of his music. Despite his worsening condition, he continued to compose, perform, and conduct, even as his hearing declined to the point of near-total deafness.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Heroic Period</strong><br>\nThe middle period of Beethoven's career is often referred to as his \"Heroic\" period, during which he composed bold and innovative works that broke new ground. Works from this time, such as his \"Eroica\" Symphony, \"Kreutzer\" Sonata, and \"Waldstein\" Sonata, are characterized by their dramatic intensity and scale, reflecting Beethoven's personal assertion of the will against adversity.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Beethoven's Late Compositions</strong><br>\nIn his later years, Beethoven's compositions became more introspective and complex, often puzzling his contemporaries. His late quartets, which were ahead of their time, pushed the boundaries of the form both technically and emotionally. Beethoven's last works, such as the Ninth Symphony with its choral finale \"Ode to Joy,\" broke new ground and set the stage for the Romantic era.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
