          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Beethoven's Innovations in Music</strong><br>\nBeethoven is credited with transforming the symphony, giving it a new scale and ambition. He also revolutionized the piano concerto and sonata, infusing them with a dramatic and heroic quality. His music's emotional depth and structural complexity were unprecedented, often featuring contrasting themes and innovative use of the developing piano's capabilities.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Beethoven's Personal Life</strong><br>\nBeethoven never married but was involved in numerous romantic relationships that remained unfulfilled. His personal life was marked by legal struggles, including a lengthy battle with his sister-in-law for the custody of his nephew. Beethoven's passionate letters reveal a man with deep emotional needs and a sense of loneliness.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nBeethoven died in 1827 after a long illness, leaving behind a body of work that would dominate the music world for centuries. His death marked the end of the Classical era and heralded the full dawn of the Romantic period in music. Beethoven's influence is immeasurable, with his compositions forming a cornerstone of the classical music canon and continuing to challenge and inspire musicians and audiences alike.</p>\n"
              },
